(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-b-v1"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam (v1, archived) — Form B","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which enzyme does each of acyclovir and oseltamivir depend on or target?","choices":["Both depend on viral thymidine kinase for activation","Both target viral neuraminidase at different sites","Acyclovir depends on viral thymidine kinase to be activated; oseltamivir targets viral neuraminidase","Acyclovir targets viral neuraminidase directly; oseltamivir depends on thymidine kinase for activation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one uses a viral enzyme to switch itself on, the other shuts a viral enzyme off. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which set of cardiovascular effects does the lecture give for acetylcholine itself?","choices":["No cardiovascular effect, since its action is confined to glands","A rise in cardiac output with a fall in heart rate","A decrease in heart rate, cardiac output and blood pressure","An increase in heart rate with a rise in blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside increased salivary, intestinal and bronchiolar secretions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"The lecture lists five bacteriostatic agents. Which group is correct?","choices":["Penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, metronidazole","Erythromycin, penicillins, sulfonamides, vancomycin, fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines, clindamycin, aminoglycosides, metronidazole, trimethoprim","Erythromycin, tetracyclines, sulfonamides, trimethoprim, clindamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are the five the lecture places on the bacteriostatic side. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which interaction does the lecture describe between benzoyl peroxide and tretinoin?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide increases tretinoin absorption to toxic levels","Tretinoin prevents benzoyl peroxide from converting to benzoic acid","The two together cause systemic hypervitaminosis A","Benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is why adapalene, which is stable with benzoyl peroxide, is highlighted separately. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which receptors does isoproterenol predominantly stimulate?","choices":["Beta-one and beta-two","Alpha-one and alpha-two","Beta-one only","Alpha-one only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it heads the beta potency order and sits at the bottom of the alpha one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called ringworm?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris","Tinea unguium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Despite the name there is no worm involved; it describes the annular appearance on the body surface. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which two properties does the lecture use to distinguish adapalene from tretinoin?","choices":["Adapalene must be applied only at night because it is photolabile","Adapalene is stable in sunlight and stable with benzoyl peroxide","Adapalene is a systemic agent and requires pregnancy testing","Adapalene is used only for psoriasis rather than acne"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it also tends to be less irritating. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which antiandrogen does the lecture name for acne?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Cyproterone","Spironolactone","Finasteride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture notes a gel formulation is available alongside oral use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is carbachol's clinical use in the lecture, and what receptor activity does it have?","choices":["Treating xerostomia, with muscarinic activity only","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis, by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","Lowering intraocular pressure in glaucoma","Stimulating an atonic bladder, with muscarinic activity only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it causes miosis and spasm of accommodation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which metabolic effect does the lecture attribute to the alpha-one blockers used for hypertension?","choices":["Raised low-density lipoprotein and triglycerides","Fasting hypoglycaemia","No metabolic effect at all","Improved lipid profiles and glucose metabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with propranolol's worsening of both. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which two groups does the lecture divide the direct-acting cholinergic agonists into?","choices":["Choline esters and naturally occurring alkaloids","Reversible and irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics and ganglionic blockers","Catecholamines and noncatecholamines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The esters are synthetic apart from acetylcholine itself. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which infection is sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim used for in both treatment and prophylaxis?","choices":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Mycobacterium avium complex","Clostridium difficile colitis","Lyme disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside urinary tract infection, bacterial prostatitis, orchitis and epididymitis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why does cocaine exaggerate epinephrine's cardiovascular actions?","choices":["It inhibits monoamine oxidase","It converts epinephrine to norepinephrine","It prevents reuptake","It increases receptor synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so the transmitter persists at the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A patient taking an aminopenicillin is also on warfarin. What does the lecture identify as the interaction?","choices":["No clinically meaningful interaction, since penicillins are renally cleared","Increased risk of tendon rupture when the two are combined","Abnormal prolongation of the prothrombin time","Reduced anticoagulant effect requiring an increased warfarin dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists anticoagulants first among the aminopenicillin interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is propranolol hazardous for a patient prone to hypoglycaemia?","choices":["It blocks glucose absorption from the gut","It masks the response to hypoglycaemia","It raises insulin secretion directly","It increases glucose uptake into muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it also decreases glycogenolysis and glucagon secretion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A patient has meningitis attributed to a herpes simplex virus. Which type does the lecture associate with the meninges?","choices":["Neither type; meningitis is caused by varicella-zoster only","Type 2","Type 1","Neither type; meningitis is a cytomegalovirus manifestation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges; type 1 covers mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Norepinephrine extravasates from a drip and the skin blanches. What does the lecture give as treatment?","choices":["Atropine","Dantrolene","Pralidoxime","Phentolamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An alpha blocker reverses the intense local vasoconstriction before the skin sloughs. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"The lecture says occlusion increases efficacy. What does it flag alongside that?","choices":["It raises the question of increased toxicity as well","It reliably shortens the required duration of therapy","It prevents the vehicle from exerting any therapeutic effect of its own","It makes regional variation in penetration irrelevant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs the efficacy gain with a query about toxicity rather than presenting it as free benefit. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is amphotericin B's mechanism of action?","choices":["It inhibits lanosterol demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis","It inhibits 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase, weakening the cell wall","It is converted to a fluorinated nucleotide by a fungal enzyme and then blocks thymidylate synthase, halting deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis","It forms channels in ergosterol-containing membranes that let potassium and magnesium leak out, and causes oxidative damage to the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The drug is derived from a soil Streptomycetaceae, and the leak is why electrolyte disturbance is such a prominent toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How does succinylcholine differ mechanistically from the nondepolarizing blockers?","choices":["It blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","It depolarizes the junction like acetylcholine but is not rapidly destroyed","It competitively prevents acetylcholine from binding without activating the receptor","It inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the junction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Continued binding leaves the receptor unable to transmit further impulses. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In which patients is propranolol contraindicated?","choices":["Those with asthma or chronic lung disease","Those with hypertension","Those with migraine","Those recovering from myocardial infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because beta-two blockade causes bronchoconstriction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What monitoring does the lecture specify for the penicillinase-resistant penicillins?","choices":["Hepatic function, complete blood count, and signs or symptoms of anaphylaxis","Renal function alone, since the class is cleared entirely by the kidney","Serum drug troughs drawn before the fourth dose","Creatine phosphokinase and muscle pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Note the dosing consideration for this class is hepatic rather than renal function, unlike most other penicillins. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient with poorly controlled diabetes develops a fungal infection. Which predisposing category does this represent?","choices":["Metabolic abnormality","Immunodeficiency","Loss of barriers","Suppression of competing organisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — diabetes is the lecture's own example for that category. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How does griseofulvin reach the site of a dermatophyte infection?","choices":["It is concentrated in sebum and delivered through the sebaceous glands","It is actively transported across the dermis by macrophages","It is applied topically and penetrates the nail plate directly","It is deposited in keratin precursor cells in skin, hair and nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is why treatment lasts as long as the keratin takes to grow out. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say an oral corticosteroid course for atopic dermatitis needs a tapering dose?","choices":["To avoid precipitating drug-induced acne","To allow the topical steroid to be started safely","To reduce the risk of secondary infection","To prevent a flare-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The taper protects against rebound rather than against withdrawal symptoms alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which adverse effects does the lecture list for albuterol?","choices":["Tremor, restlessness and anxiety","Sedation, dry mouth and constipation","Bradycardia and bronchoconstriction","Miosis, salivation and diarrhoea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, the familiar beta-two agonist profile. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic does the lecture identify as approved for axillary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Scopolamine","Glycopyrronium","Dicyclomine","Tiotropium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, approved in 2018 for that indication. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What must a patient starting metronidazole be counselled to avoid, and why?","choices":["Sun exposure, because of photosensitivity","Ethanol, because of a disulfiram-like reaction","Dairy products, because of chelation reducing absorption","Tyramine-containing foods, because of hypertensive crisis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the class's signature counselling point, alongside headache, nausea and vomiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What does the lecture tell patients to do after handling a scopolamine patch?","choices":["Avoid showering for twenty-four hours so the patch adheres","Apply the patch to the same site each time to maintain levels","Take an antihistamine alongside it to prevent sedation","Wash their hands thoroughly afterwards"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a small instruction that prevents an alarming symptom. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does the lecture split topical against systemic therapy in acne?","choices":["Topical for adults, systemic for adolescents","Topical for the face, systemic for the trunk","Topical for mild to moderate disease, systemic for moderate to severe","Topical for inflammatory disease, systemic for comedonal disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severity is what moves a patient onto systemic therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which mycoses does the lecture place in the subcutaneous category?","choices":["Chromomycosis, Pseudallescheriasis and Sporotrichosis","Tinea pedis, tinea cruris and tinea corporis","Aspergillosis, cryptococcosis and histoplasmosis","Oral and vaginal candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the middle tier, affecting skin layers rather than the surface or the internal organs. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which systemic corticosteroid does the lecture explicitly exclude from causing drug-induced acne?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the eruption is not seen with hydrocortisone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which set matches atropine's adverse effects in the lecture?","choices":["Sedation, constipation and rebound hypertension","Dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and constipation","Salivation, lacrimation, diarrhoea and bronchospasm","Fasciculations, weakness and hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with bradycardia at lower doses and tachycardia at higher ones. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which viruses does oseltamivir target, and in what season?","choices":["Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster, circulating year-round","Cytomegalovirus in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B, in a season running roughly October to March","Influenza A only, year-round"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and resistance has been becoming a problem over time. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Where does the lecture place alpha-one as against alpha-two receptors?","choices":["Alpha-one postsynaptically, alpha-two presynaptically","Alpha-one presynaptically and alpha-two postsynaptically","Both exclusively on the postsynaptic membrane","Both exclusively within the central nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and that presynaptic location is what makes an alpha-two agonist reduce sympathetic outflow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which two conditions does varicella-zoster virus cause?","choices":["Retinitis and colitis","Infectious mononucleosis and hepatitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — primary infection and later reactivation of the same virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which organisms is flucytosine indicated against?","choices":["Helminths including roundworm and whipworm","Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida","Dermatophytes of the nails and scalp","Aspergillus and the Zygomycetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is given in combination rather than alone because resistance develops readily. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which manifestations does the lecture attribute to cytomegalovirus?","choices":["Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration","Influenza-like illness with fever and myalgia","Retinitis, oesophagitis and colitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they occur chiefly in transplant recipients and other immunocompromised patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which vehicle does the lecture place at the most drying end of its range, and which at the least?","choices":["Tinctures are the most drying and ointments the least","Ointments are the most drying and tinctures the least","Creams are the most drying and gels the least","Powders are the most drying and lotions the least"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The list runs tinctures, wet dressings, lotions, gels, aerosols, powders, pastes, creams, foams, ointments. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is commonly called jock itch?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Tinea pedis","Tinea unguium","Tinea cruris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, affecting the groin. The azoles are indicated across this whole tinea group. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which receptor does phenylephrine act on, and what happens to the heart rate?","choices":["Beta-two, with no rate change","Alpha-two, with reduced sympathetic outflow","Alpha-one, with reflex bradycardia","Beta-one, with tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It has no direct cardiac action; the slowing is a baroreceptor reflex. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"An immunocompromised patient has systemic aspergillosis that has not responded to itraconazole or amphotericin B. Which class does the lecture position for this?","choices":["Echinocandins","Polyenes","Allylamines","Mitotic inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — their listed role is largely salvage, in refractory aspergillosis, oesophageal candidiasis, and febrile neutropenia not responding to antibiotics. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which pharmacokinetic properties does the lecture list under drug factors when choosing a regimen?","choices":["Onset, peak, duration, and half-life","Bioavailability, protein binding, volume of distribution, and clearance","Bactericidal activity, time-dependent killing, and concentration-dependent killing","Absorption, distribution, penetration into body compartments, and elimination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penetration into compartments is called out separately, which is why central nervous system penetration recurs for individual classes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which effect do the alpha-adrenergic blocking agents share, per the lecture?","choices":["Reflex tachycardia","Reflex bradycardia","Bronchoconstriction","Reduced aqueous humour production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and their effects on blood pressure are more profound when the patient stands. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A lesion is oozing, vesicular and crusting. Which end of the vehicle range does the lecture pair with that presentation?","choices":["Vehicle choice is not linked to lesion morphology","The drying end of the range","The least drying end of the range","The middle of the range, regardless of the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oozing, vesiculation and crusting are matched to drying vehicles; scaling, lichenification and xerosis to the least drying. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happens with nicotine at autonomic ganglia?","choices":["It permanently destroys ganglionic receptors","It depolarizes the ganglia, first stimulating and then paralysing them","It competitively and reversibly blocks them from the outset","It has no ganglionic action and acts only in the central nervous system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the stimulation raises blood pressure, heart rate, peristalsis and secretions before the fall. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why does the lecture prefer a selective beta-two agonist for chronic asthma over epinephrine?","choices":["Greater potency at beta-two receptors","Oral availability","Absence of any tremor","Longer duration of action"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine reverses acute bronchospasm; albuterol covers chronic treatment. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which tetracycline precautions relate to children and pregnancy?","choices":["Cartilage damage — avoid under 18 years old","Kernicterus from bilirubin displacement — avoid in the first 30 days of life and throughout the third trimester of pregnancy","Grey baby syndrome — avoid in all trimesters","Discoloration of teeth and depression of skeletal growth — avoid under 8 years old and in the second and third trimesters"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both follow from the drug binding calcium in developing bone and teeth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Under drug factors for choosing a regimen, which toxicities does the lecture list as the organ systems to consider?","choices":["Hematologic, renal, hepatic, endocrine, vascular, lymphatic","Gastrointestinal, integument, hematologic, central nervous system, hepatic, renal","Cardiac, pulmonary, endocrine, musculoskeletal, ocular, and dermatologic systems","Gastrointestinal, renal, hepatic, ocular, reproductive, immunologic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Six systems, and they map onto the monitoring parameters repeated for individual classes later in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is timolol's ocular action, and what is it used for?","choices":["It reduces aqueous humour production","It causes miosis, for chronic open-angle glaucoma","It causes mydriasis, for examination of the fundus","It reduces corneal oedema, for keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a different route to lowering pressure than pilocarpine's. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Why did aminoglycoside dosing move from every 8 hours to every 24 hours?","choices":["Once-daily dosing eliminates the need for therapeutic drug monitoring","Renal clearance is so rapid that more frequent dosing has no effect","They show a post-antibiotic effect, so killing persists after the level falls, and concentration-dependent killing favours a large single dose","They show time-dependent killing, so a longer interval keeps the level above the minimal inhibitory concentration for a greater share of the dosing period"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the clearest clinical application of the concentration-dependent pattern from earlier in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What do ganglionic blockers do, and why are they rarely used?","choices":["They block alpha and beta receptors simultaneously","They block nicotinic receptors at sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia","They block muscarinic receptors only, sparing the sympathetic system","They act at the neuromuscular junction to relax skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and blocking everything at once is precisely why they are rarely used clinically. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which polyene is used specifically for oral candidiasis?","choices":["Natamycin","Caspofungin","Nystatin","Amphotericin B deoxycholate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, available as oral preparations and a topical powder, with systemic effects limited by poor absorption. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for acyclovir?","choices":["Confusion, ataxia, seizures and coma","Peripheral neuropathy and tendon rupture","Visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients","Seizures, delirium and tremor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which patient should not be given a topical retinoid, per the lecture?","choices":["A patient over sixty years of age","A patient who is pregnant","A patient with a history of eczema","A patient taking an oral contraceptive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to avoid retinoids during pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient presents on day 4 of an influenza-like illness. What does the lecture imply about oseltamivir?","choices":["Treatment is equally effective at any point in the illness","Acyclovir should be substituted","The 48-hour window for starting treatment has passed","Treatment should be started at a doubled dose to compensate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the drug blocks release of new virus rather than clearing existing virus, starting late has little to act on. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"What are the black box warnings for the polymyxins?","choices":["Ototoxicity, red man syndrome, and phlebitis","Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, and neuromuscular blockade","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system effects","Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome, and hypertensive crisis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the only warnings the lecture labels explicitly as black box, which is why they are worth holding separately from ordinary adverse effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A patient develops athlete's foot after prolonged use of occlusive footwear. Which predisposing factor is this?","choices":["Loss of barriers","Immunodeficiency","Metabolic abnormality","A warm moist environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture's own examples for that category are diaper rash and athlete's foot. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which antibiotic classes bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, and which bind the 30S?","choices":["50S — vancomycin, daptomycin; 30S — linezolid, tigecycline","50S — macrolides, clindamycin, chloramphenicol, streptogramins; 30S — aminoglycosides, tetracyclines","50S — aminoglycosides, tetracyclines; 30S — macrolides, clindamycin, chloramphenicol, streptogramins","50S — fluoroquinolones, metronidazole; 30S — penicillins, cephalosporins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial ribosome is 70S against the mammalian 80S, which is the basis for selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which uses does the lecture list for neostigmine?","choices":["Slowing the progression of Alzheimer disease","Treating an overdose of an anticholinergic drug","Stimulating the bladder and gut, and reversing competitive blockade","Diagnosing myasthenia gravis and assessing cholinesterase therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it can stimulate contractility before paralysing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"}]};
